WEBVTT

00:00:00.680 --> 00:00:03.260
Nick Ward didn't just convince strangers to hand him money on the street.
尼克·沃ard（Nick Ward）不只是在街头说服陌生人把钱交给他。

00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:06.820
He talked them into going to their bank machines if they didn't have money on them.
如果他们身上没带钱，他就说服他们去银行取款机取钱。

00:00:07.110 --> 00:00:11.080
He talked them into going back to their apartments if they didn't have their cash card with them.
如果他们没带现金卡，他就说服他们回公寓去取。

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:15.280
Some of the people he talked into giving him money two and three times.
他甚至能说服一些人给他掏过两三次钱。

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:19.700
And when they left him, a lot of these people hugged him.
当这些人离开他时，很多人还拥抱了他。

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:20.840
They said, God bless you.
他们说，上帝保佑你。

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:21.980
They felt good about themselves.
他们对自己的感觉好极了。

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:25.940
They'd been conned, and they never felt better.
他们明明被骗了，却感觉前所未有的好。

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:28.140
There's an artist for you.
这可真是一位艺术家。

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:28.840
That's a gift.
这绝对是一种天赋。

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:40.180
Well, from WBEZ Chicago and Public Radio International, it is This American Life.
这里是来自芝加哥WBEZ电台和国际公共广播电台的《这一生》（This American Life）。

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:40.900
I'm Ira Glass.
我是艾拉·格拉斯（Ira Glass）。

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:45.420
Each week on our program, of course, we choose some theme, bring you a variety of stories on that theme.
每周我们的节目都会选定一个主题，为您带来围绕该主题的各种故事。

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:48.720
Today's program, how to take money from strangers.
今天的节目是：如何从陌生人那里拿钱。

00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:53.840
We bring you three stories today about nerve and entrepreneurial spirit and everything that makes this country great.
今天我们为您带来三个关于胆识、企业家精神以及造就这个伟大国家的一切的故事。

00:00:55.390 --> 00:01:00.380
Plus, of course, lying and deception and everything that makes it a more complicated place to live.
当然，还有谎言与欺骗，以及让这个世界变得更加复杂的一切。

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:02.180
We have three case studies today.
今天我们有三个案例研究。

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:07.000
One on the street, one in the salons of the wealthy, one on the phone.
一个在街头，一个在富人的沙龙里，还有一个在电话里。

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:22.000
Back one of our program, St. Nick, a con man who makes money partly by making people feel like they are good people living in a good country.
节目的第一部分，“圣尼克”，一个骗子，他赚钱的部分方式是让人们觉得自己是生活在一个好国家里的好人。

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:25.520
Act two, what makes Grammy run?
第二部分，是什么在驱动格拉姆？

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:35.220
At the other end of the economic spectrum, a legitimate businessman who makes his living by selling, I have to say, a very similar picture of the world to his customers, who are the very rich.
在经济光谱的另一端，有一位合法的商人，他靠向客户兜售对世界的看法维生——我得说，这种世界观非常眼熟——而他的客户正是那些极富之人。

00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:36.760
That story from Sarah Val.
这个故事由莎拉·瓦尔（Sarah Val）讲述。

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:39.900
Act three, futures market.
第三部分，期货市场。

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:48.390
The true story of how writer Stephen Glass took money from strangers by selling hope and fear when he got a job as a telephone psychic.
真实的故事：作家史蒂芬·格拉斯（Stephen Glass）在找到一份电话心理咨询师的工作后，如何通过兜售希望和恐惧从陌生人那里获取金钱。

00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:51.400
A rare look inside that very American business.
带您罕见地一窥那个极具美国特色的行业。

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:52.740
Stay with us.
请继续收听。

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:04.340
Act One, St. Nick.
第一部分，圣尼克。

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:11.980
Well, when this American Life producer, Nancy Updake, started working on this particular story, she was making phone calls to Philadelphia, where Nick Ward ran his con.
当《这一生》的制作人南希·乌普代克（Nancy Updake）开始着手制作这个特别的故事时，她给费城打了很多电话，尼克·沃德就是在那里行骗的。

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:18.540
And she would be on the phone with friends of hers in Philly, and she would mention the story, and they'd say, oh, that guy, that guy, he took money from me.
她跟费城的朋友通电话时提到了这个故事，他们就会说：“哦，那家伙，那家伙，他骗过我的钱。”

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:21.420
This happened over and over and over.
这种情况一次又一次、没完没了地发生。

00:02:22.540 --> 00:02:28.380
Nick Ward worked the streets so long and so effectively that everyone seemed to have been conned by him or knew somebody who had been conned by him.
尼克·沃德在街头作案时间之长、手段之高明，以至于每个人似乎都曾被他骗过，或者认识被他骗过的人。

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:36.040
And a couple of days into her research, Nancy suddenly remembered that back when she lived in Philadelphia years ago, she was conned by him.
在搜集资料几天后，南希突然想起来，多年前她住在费城时，自己也被他骗过。

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:38.050
Here's her story.
以下是她的故事。

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:44.800
There's an unfortunate twist to this story, which is that some of the key characters are actually dead or lost.
这个故事有一个不幸的转折，那就是一些关键人物实际上已经去世或失联了。

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:51.460
One was a bemused judge who said on one of Nick Ward's frequent visits to his courtroom, you have a brain that's beautiful.
其中一位是困惑的法官，他在尼克·沃德频繁出入他的法庭时说道：“你有一个美丽的头脑。”

00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:54.200
But that brain has been turned to the con.
“但这个头脑却用到了骗术上。”

00:02:55.740 --> 00:03:02.120
Then there was a little old lady in New Hampshire who got dozens and dozens of phone calls from strangers in Philadelphia who were conned by Nick Ward.
然后是新罕布什尔州的一位老太太，她接到了费城陌生人打来的几十通甚至上百通电话，这些人都是被尼克·沃德骗的。

00:03:03.700 --> 00:03:07.040
And then there's Nick Ward himself, who is nowhere to be found.
还有尼克·沃德本人，他现在不知所踪。

00:03:12.260 --> 00:03:16.020
Okay, Center City, Philadelphia's charmingly small downtown area.
好了，市中心（Center City），费城迷人的小范围市中心。

00:03:16.750 --> 00:03:53.600
picture a skyline of about four buildings and then around those 20 square blocks or so of law offices hotels and those kind of little boutiques where you have to be buzzed in this is where a lot of the city's white-collar professionals work and it's also where Nick Ward worked it's where he conned and this is a partial list two assistant district attorneys three metal salesmen an architect a university professor several doctors a folk singer a medical student a geologist the the secretary of the Delaware Valley Association of Railroad Passengers, and, in another part of the city, the chief of the criminal division of the U.S.
想象一下，天际线大概只有四栋建筑，然后周围大约有20个街区，里面分布着律师事务所、酒店，以及那种需要按门铃才能进去的小佳品店，这里是该市许多白领专业人士工作的地方，也是尼克·沃德工作的地方，是他行骗的地方，这是一份部分名单：两名助理地区检察官、三名金属推销员、一名建筑师、一名大学教授、几名医生、一名民谣歌手、一名医学生、一名地质学家、特拉华山谷铁路乘客协会的秘书，以及在城市的另一个地方，美国联邦检察官办公室刑事部门的主任，他最著名的战绩是瓦解了统治费城和新泽西州南部多年的小尼基·斯卡尔福（Little Nicky Scarfo）黑帮。

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:59.980
Attorney's Office, best known for dismantling the Little Nicky Scarfo mob that had run Philadelphia and southern New Jersey for years.
办公室，最著名的事迹是瓦解了统治费城及新泽西州南部多年的小尼基·斯卡尔福（Little Nicky Scarfo）黑帮团伙。

00:04:06.820 --> 00:04:16.720
I was walking down Market Street, the main street in Philadelphia, on my way to drop off some things to the Salvation Army, believe it or not.
信不信由你，我当时正走在费城的主干道市场街（Market Street）上，准备去救世军（Salvation Army）送点东西。

00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:20.340
I was the perfect candidate for this.
我绝对是干这事的完美人选。

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:23.560
This is Bill Marsh, an art director at the Inquirer.
这是《问询报》（Inquirer）的美术总监比尔·马什（Bill Marsh）。

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:29.880
Ward found him in the same ideal state he found dozens of others at a moment where they felt full of the spirit of giving.
沃德发现他时，正处于他发现其他几十人时的那种理想状态——当时他们心中正涌动着奉献精神。

00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:36.460
He caught a lawyer on her way back from a soup kitchen on Thanksgiving Day, another woman leaving church services on Christmas Eve.
他在感恩节那天抓住了一位刚从救济厨房出来的女律师，另一个圣诞节前夕刚做完礼拜的女人也被他抓住了。

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:41.200
But Ward also had a gift for spotting the people who give all year round.
但沃德也有一种天赋，能一眼看出那些全年都在奉献的人。

00:04:41.960 --> 00:04:44.610
Gary Glenn is an editor for the American College of Physicians.
加里·格伦（Gary Glenn）是《美国内科医师学会杂志》的一名编辑。

00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:49.000
I was out at lunch, and I was walking through the underground concourse at Suburban Station.
我当时正出去吃午饭，正走过郊区车站（Suburban Station）地下的室内通道。

00:04:50.540 --> 00:04:56.160
And my wife and I have volunteered in homeless shelters and generally give the Panhandlers a lot.
我和妻子经常在流浪汉收容所做志愿者，平时也经常给街头乞讨者很多钱。

00:04:57.640 --> 00:05:08.880
Gary and Bill, two men who were strangers before their respective Nick Ward experiences, experiences will now perform a duet for you about how Nick Ward talked them into giving him a total of $135.
加里和比尔，这两个在各自遭遇尼克·沃德之前互不相识的男人，现在将为您合唱一曲，讲讲尼克·沃德是如何说服他们一共交出135美元的。

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:14.520
He said he was a college kid from Boston studying fashion design.
他说自己是个来自波士顿、学服装设计的大学生。

00:05:14.860 --> 00:05:17.520
He'd come down here for an interview at a department store.
他来这儿是为了去一家百货公司面试。

00:05:17.960 --> 00:05:19.140
He's a fashion designer.
他是个时装设计师。

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:24.740
He was in Philadelphia to present some of his designs and photography to one of the big department stores here.
他来费城是为了向这里的一家大百货公司展示他的一些设计和摄影作品。

00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:28.360
A gypsy cab driver had He had taken his garment bag and portfolio.
一个吉普赛出租车司机……拿走了他的服装袋和作品集。

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:29.640
He didn't have his check.
他没有支票。

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:30.560
He didn't have an ATM card.
他也没有自动取款机卡。

00:05:31.580 --> 00:05:33.600
A gypsy cab, as he put it.
用他自己的话说，是一辆吉普赛出租车。

00:05:35.020 --> 00:05:38.600
Made off with all his stuff, camera equipment, photography, everything.
把他所有的东西都卷走了，相机设备、摄影作品，什么都没了。

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:40.460
He was left stranded here.
他被困在这里了。

00:05:41.060 --> 00:05:42.060
He called the cops.
他报了警。

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:43.200
He'd been to Traveler's Aid.
他去过旅行者援助协会（Traveler's Aid）。

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:44.900
Yeah, it was a Traveler's Aid office.
对，就是一个旅行者援助协会的办公室。

00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:46.840
He wanted directions to the Traveler's Aid office.
他想问去旅行者援助协会办公室怎么走。

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:50.320
And I don't know how to put this.
我不知道该怎么说。

00:05:50.320 --> 00:06:00.520
he played upon all kinds of racial guilt that one might feel.
他利用了人们可能会感受到的各种种族负罪感。

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:04.020
He's black and everyone he approached is white.
他是黑人，而他接近的每个人都是白人。

00:06:04.700 --> 00:06:06.200
Oh, of course that fed into it.
哦，这当然起作用了。

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:16.420
I mean, you look at all the people that Nick picked, they were all white, mostly suburban, middle class, the same people who make the United Way donations or give some money to the church clothing drive.
我的意思是，你看尼克挑中的所有人，全都是白人，大部分是郊区中产阶级，也就是那些会向联合劝募会（United Way）捐款或给教堂衣物捐赠箱捐钱的同一拨人。

00:06:18.320 --> 00:06:19.960
Let's add an alto voice to this duet.
让我们在这个二重唱里加入一个女低音。

00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:24.120
This is Ginny Wigand, the reporter who broke the story about Nick Ward in the Philadelphia Inquirer.
这位是詹妮·威甘德（Ginny Wigand），也就是在《费城问询报》上独家爆料尼克·沃德事件的记者。

00:06:25.080 --> 00:06:31.980
When people would kind of walk quickly away from him, he would call out after them, don't shun me just because I'm a black male.
当人们想要快步走开时，他会在后面大喊：“别因为我是黑人男性就避开我。”

00:06:32.910 --> 00:06:36.040
And of course, some people would stop in their tracks and just feel terrible.
当然，有些人会停下脚步，感到内疚不已。

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:38.140
And as soon as they stopped, he had them.
而只要他们一停下来，他就把他们拿下了。

00:06:42.820 --> 00:06:58.980
He would say that when he arrived in Philadelphia, he was lost and ended up near the University of Pennsylvania campus and some white fraternity brothers were nasty to him and called him racial names, which, of course, made everyone feel terrible.
他会说，他刚到费城时迷路了，结果走到宾夕法尼亚大学校园附近，一些白人兄弟会成员对他很不友好，还用带有种族歧视的词汇骂他，这当然让每个人都感到难受极了。

00:06:59.180 --> 00:07:04.660
And he'd say, can you believe that in the city of brotherly love that they would treat a black man like that and someone like me?
他会说：“你敢相信，在兄弟之爱之城（City of Brotherly Love），他们竟然会这样对待一个黑人和像我这样的人吗？”

00:07:04.840 --> 00:07:06.140
And he was very well-dressed.
而且他穿着得体。

00:07:06.200 --> 00:07:08.120
He was clean-shaven.
他刮了胡子。

00:07:08.120 --> 00:07:10.640
He had a nice wool sweater, leather jacket on.
他穿着一件好看的羊毛衫，外面套着皮夹克。

00:07:10.900 --> 00:07:15.560
He would offer to give people his watch, you know, as proof that he was the real thing.
他会主动把自己的手表送给别人，你知道的，以此证明他是认真的。

00:07:15.900 --> 00:08:01.140
um so and no one ever took it so he could offer it every time well i guess people felt that made them feel terrible to i'm having these doubts about him and look is as evidence of his goodwill and good faith he's offering me this nice watch and so no no one ever took it every single suggestion that I made to him to get help was he said he'd already tried or you know they're all racist you know he I don't I didn't want to be one of those people certainly didn't want to be thought of as racist because I'm not.
嗯，所以……而且从来没有人拿过，所以他每次都可以拿出来当筹码。好吧，我猜人们觉得这也让他们感觉糟透了，我竟然对产生这种怀疑，你看，作为他善意和诚意的证明，他把这块好表都送给我了，所以……不，从来没有人拿过。我向他提出的每一个求助建议，他都说已经试过了，或者你知道的，他们全都是种族主义者。你知道，我……我不想成为那种人，绝对不想被当成种族主义者，因为我不是。

00:08:01.920 --> 00:08:03.910
And that's what you bought for $35.
而这就是你花35美元买到的东西。

00:08:07.500 --> 00:08:08.620
Feeling good about myself.
感觉自己是个好人。

00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:10.580
You get to be the non-racist.
你成了一个没有种族歧视倾向的人。

00:08:12.540 --> 00:08:13.720
Yeah, I suppose.
对，我想是的。

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:25.940
What he was asking for was the price of a train ticket back to Boston.
他要的其实只是一张回波士顿的车票钱。

00:08:27.400 --> 00:08:53.220
and i doubt i would have financed that even if i'd had the money i gave him everything i had which was 35 dollars and i said listen i can give you 20 bucks but then he said listen if i have to tell my story to like five more people to get the money i need i'll be here all day i don't want to be in philadelphia tonight with no place to go i think all right that sounds logical so i went to to an ATM machine, took out a hundred and gave it to him.
而且就算我有钱，我当时也未必会资助他。我给了我身上仅有的全部35美元，我说，听着，我只能给你20块。但接着他说，听着，如果我非得把我的遭遇跟另外差不多五个人讲才能凑够需要的钱，那我一整天都得耗在这了，我可不想今晚流落费城街头无处可去。我想，好吧，这倒也合情合理。于是我走到自动取款机前，取了一百块给了他。

00:08:53.960 --> 00:09:06.740
And I honestly believed, not entirely, I didn't think that there was a possibility that this was insincere, but I honestly expected to get a check and a call from him.
我当时真的相信——倒也不是百分之百，我并非完全没想过这可能是假的——但我真的期待能收到他的支票和电话。

00:09:07.900 --> 00:09:10.060
You know, of course, that Nick did not call.
当然，你们都知道，尼克并没有打电话来。

00:09:11.380 --> 00:09:15.640
But perhaps you're not fully understanding how thoroughly Nick made his marks believe in him.
但也许你还没有完全理解，尼克让他的目标对他是多么深信不疑。

00:09:16.180 --> 00:09:17.100
Let me tell you this story.
让我给你讲讲这个故事。

00:09:18.140 --> 00:09:22.720
Several months after Bill was conned, he ran into Nick while Nick was in mid-scam with someone else.
在比尔被骗几个月后，他偶遇了正在对另一个人行骗的尼克。

00:09:23.810 --> 00:09:25.200
I made a fool of myself.
我当时简直像个傻子。

00:09:25.460 --> 00:09:27.020
I came off looking like the lunatic.
我看起来像个疯子。

00:09:28.300 --> 00:09:36.500
I was walking home from work late one night, and I saw Nick with a young woman deep in conversation.
那天深夜我正下班步行回家，看到尼克正和一个年轻女性相谈甚欢。

00:09:36.540 --> 00:09:52.780
conversation, without really thinking, I went up to him with a very badly composed and hysterical outburst, something like, do you know who this guy is?
交谈着，没怎么多想，我就冲了过去，情绪失控地大喊大叫，语无伦次，大概是这样的话：“你知道这人是谁吗？”

00:09:53.370 --> 00:09:57.980
No, as a matter of fact, the first words out of my mouth were, do not give this man any money.
不，事实上，我嘴里蹦出的第一句话是：“千万别给这人钱。”

00:09:58.040 --> 00:09:58.900
Do you know who he is?
“你知道他是谁吗？”

00:09:59.260 --> 00:10:00.460
This is Nick Jerome Ward.
“这是尼克·杰罗姆·沃德（Nick Jerome Ward）。”

00:10:00.620 --> 00:10:01.560
He's a scam artist.
“他是个骗子。”

00:10:01.720 --> 00:10:02.840
You don't want to give him any money.
“你可千万别给他钱。”

00:10:02.900 --> 00:10:05.780
I'm telling you, whatever he's telling you is not true.
“我告诉你，他跟你说的全都是假的。”

00:10:06.600 --> 00:10:08.660
And I went on like that for a couple of minutes.
我像这样喋喋不休了好几分钟。

00:10:08.860 --> 00:10:21.280
She seemed rather stunned and he stood there very calmly and I would say professionally and said to her, I don't know what he's talking about.
她看起来相当震惊，而他则非常冷静，可以说是表现得很专业，对她说：“我不知道他在胡说八道些什么。”

00:10:21.500 --> 00:10:23.880
He's not telling the truth.
“他说的不是实话。”

00:10:23.980 --> 00:10:24.760
I don't know this guy.
“我不认识这家伙。”

00:10:26.260 --> 00:11:01.820
And to my surprise and dismay she seemed to believe him more than she believed me i was i was the nut bar in that here's an interesting question is what nick was doing illegal lying is not against the law Telling someone you want money for a train ticket and then buying yourself dinner is also not against the law.
令我惊讶和沮丧的是，她似乎比相信我更相信他。当时我才是个不折不扣的怪胎。这里有个有趣的问题：尼克的所作所为违法吗？撒谎并不违反法律。告诉别人你需要钱买车票，然后拿去给自己买晚饭吃，同样也不违法。

00:11:03.200 --> 00:11:09.680
Nick's problem was he told people he was going to pay them back, and he just made that promise to too many people.
尼克的问题在于，他告诉别人他会把钱还给他们，结果他把这个承诺许给了太多人。

00:11:10.060 --> 00:11:14.860
This is how Matthew Perks, the assistant district attorney who supervised Nick's prosecution, saw it.
负责监督尼克起诉工作的助理地区检察官马修·佩克斯（Matthew Perks）是这样看的。

00:11:15.060 --> 00:11:26.940
If the cases were each looked at individually, he could probably mount a defense to maybe one or a few that we would be unable to prove that he did not intend to pay the money back.
如果把这些案子单独拿出来看，对于其中一件或几件，他大概都能进行辩护，导致我们无法证明他当时并不打算还钱。

00:11:27.490 --> 00:11:31.880
Because that was the only way that it was illegal, as if he said, I will pay you back.
因为这是证明其违法的唯一途径，除非他说过“我会还你钱”。

00:11:32.180 --> 00:11:32.620
That's correct.
没错。

00:11:32.680 --> 00:11:42.480
We would have to create an inference in the mind of whoever was deciding, a judge at that point, that he, beyond a reasonable doubt, didn't intend to pay the money back.
我们必须在做出裁决的人（当时是一名法官）心中建立起一种推论，即他超越合理怀疑地根本不打算还钱。

00:11:53.200 --> 00:11:58.760
For someone whose con was so smooth and so well thought out, Nick made one big mistake.
对于一个骗术如此天衣无缝、思虑如此周全的骗子来说，尼克犯了一个大错。

00:11:59.000 --> 00:12:00.640
A really strange mistake, actually.
说实话，是一个非常奇怪的错误。

00:12:01.160 --> 00:12:07.780
The phone number he gave Bill was the same one he gave Gary, which was also the same one he gave everyone.
他给比尔留的电话号码，和给加里的完全一样，也是他留给所有人的那个号码。

00:12:08.720 --> 00:12:22.260
And it turned out to be the actual unlisted phone number of this woman who was a retired editor of an outdoor magazine, living in New Hampshire, originally from Finland, who was home sick with cancer and started getting all these weird calls from people in Philadelphia.
结果证明，那其实是一位女士未公开的私人电话号码。她是一位户外杂志的退休编辑，住在新罕布什尔州，祖籍芬兰，当时正因患病在家休养，结果开始接到所有这些来自费城的陌生人的古怪电话。

00:12:23.260 --> 00:12:30.440
And after a while, she just started taking down their names and phone numbers and putting them in touch with each other, until finally she had this database of about 150 people.
过了一段时间，她索性开始记下他们的姓名和电话号码，并让他们互相联系，直到最后她手头上掌握了一个大约有150人的数据库。

00:12:32.200 --> 00:12:39.480
So Ginny started to write a story about Nick for the Inquirer, and she called this woman up and got all these names, which made it this huge story.
于是詹妮开始为《问询报》撰写一篇关于尼克的报道，她打电话给这位女士，拿到了所有这些名字，这使得这篇报道成了一个轰动的大新闻。

00:12:39.920 --> 00:12:45.320
It came out in the Sunday paper, got the front page, top of the fold, and everything was put in motion.
新闻登在了周日报纸的头版头条、折页上方，一切都顺理成章地运转起来。

00:12:45.480 --> 00:12:51.230
The police, who had actually known about Nick, but who'd been busy with more serious crimes, got embarrassed, and arrested him.
原本其实知道尼克其人、但一直在忙于更严重罪行的警察感到脸上无光，于是把他给抓了。

00:12:51.980 --> 00:12:54.420
Nick ended up in court and eventually went to prison.
尼克最终上了法庭，并最终进了监狱。

00:12:55.620 --> 00:13:01.320
But what's interesting is that along the way, there was all this outrage from the people he'd conned.
但有趣的是，在此期间，那些被他骗过的人表现出了极大的愤怒。

00:13:01.700 --> 00:13:02.440
Truly, outrage.
真的是出离愤怒。

00:13:03.760 --> 00:13:09.360
The public defender who worked Nick's case said to me, Look, I've been a public defender for 20 years.
负责尼克一案的公设辩护人对我说：“听着，我当了20年公设辩护人。”

00:13:09.940 --> 00:13:19.820
I have worked with rape victims, victims of terrible, violent crimes, and I have never seen the kind of vitriol from any of those victims that I saw from Nick Ward's victims.
“我接触过强奸受害者、可怕的暴力犯罪受害者，但我从未在那些受害者身上见过我在尼克·沃德受害者身上看到的这种恶毒痛恨。”

00:13:21.260 --> 00:13:24.200
Gary said he was shocked by the mood in the courtroom when he was there.
加里说，当他身处法庭时，对法庭里的气氛感到震惊。

00:13:24.740 --> 00:13:29.820
All these well-dressed, well-spoken sort of do-gooder yuppies turned into this jeering mob.
所有这些衣着光鲜、谈吐文雅的伪善雅皮士，都变成了一群叫骂的暴民。

00:13:30.420 --> 00:13:38.140
When Nick was brought in, a lot of people started taunting him, saying, hey, Nick, what have you got to say for yourself now?
当尼克被带进来时，许多人开始嘲弄他，说道：“嘿，尼克，现在你有什么好说的？”

00:13:38.400 --> 00:13:40.280
Oh, now he's all quiet, doesn't have anything to say.
“哦，现在他倒挺安静，什么话也没有了。”

00:13:40.980 --> 00:13:51.280
and I was looking around at the people who had been scammed by him and I don't think there was anybody there who couldn't have done without the amount of money that they gave him.
我环顾四周，看着那些被他骗过的人，我觉得那里没有一个人是给不起他那些钱的。

00:13:51.760 --> 00:13:57.340
And there were people there saying, yeah, I gave him 25 bucks and they were mad as if they'd given him their daughter or something.
现场有人说，没错，我给了他25美元，他们那股愤怒的劲头，就好像把自己的女儿嫁了出去或者别的什么似的。

00:13:57.480 --> 00:13:58.550
It was really unbalanced.
这真的很失衡。

00:14:02.340 --> 00:14:12.080
I'm not defending Nick at all but just the vindictiveness of the people in the court that day I thought was way out of proportion.
我一点也没有为尼克辩护的意思，但那天法庭上那些人的记仇心理，我觉得实在是有点过头了。

00:14:19.480 --> 00:14:21.080
Why were people so mad?
大家为什么这么生气？

00:14:23.220 --> 00:14:25.620
Maybe it was because Nick didn't just talk them out of some cash.
也许是因为尼克骗走的不仅仅是他们的现金。

00:14:26.000 --> 00:14:28.160
He made his marks identify with him.
他让受害者对他产生了认同感。

00:14:28.740 --> 00:14:39.100
He convinced them that he was a member of their club, the club of people who go to college and have job interviews and careers, and who every once in a while just have some random bad luck.
他让自己看起来像是他们那个圈子的一员——那个上大学、参加工作面试、有着职业生涯，并且偶尔会遭遇一些倒霉事的普通人圈子。

00:14:40.720 --> 00:14:44.720
People looked at him and thought, this could be my kid, or even, this could be me.
人们看着他心想，这可能就是我的孩子，甚至觉得，这可能就是我自己。

00:14:45.960 --> 00:14:46.160
Here's Gary.
加里是这么说的。

00:14:47.080 --> 00:15:00.330
I think every other panhandler you see on the street, you know, oh God, they're a junkie, they're an alcoholic, or whatever, and you kind of give money to them out of a sense of pity, and almost condescension.
我觉得你在街上看到的其他乞丐，你都会觉得，天哪，他们是个瘾君子、酒鬼或者别的什么，你给他们钱纯粹出于同情，甚至带有一种居高临下的姿态。

00:15:00.880 --> 00:15:02.580
Yeah, brother, here's a quarter.
行吧，兄弟，拿去个硬币。

00:15:02.690 --> 00:15:08.360
But Nick was hitting you as an equal or as someone who was trying to work his way up to be your equal.
但尼克打击你的时候，是以一个平等的人的身份，或者是一个正努力提升自己、想要和你平起平坐的人的身份。

00:15:08.980 --> 00:15:10.680
And I thought, yeah, I've got to give the guy a chance.
当时我想，好吧，我得给这伙计一个机会。

00:15:11.190 --> 00:15:12.140
I've got to give the guy a chance.
我得给这伙计一个机会。

00:15:22.920 --> 00:15:24.760
How do you get money from a stranger?
你怎么能从一个陌生人手里弄到钱呢？

00:15:26.420 --> 00:15:27.240
You become a salesman.
你得成为一名推销员。

00:15:28.080 --> 00:15:29.960
First you flatter, then you sell.
先奉承，再推销。

00:15:29.960 --> 00:15:30.460
well.
没错。

00:15:31.100 --> 00:15:33.100
Nick's victims liked the image of themselves.
尼克的受害者们喜欢自己当时的那个形象。

00:15:33.540 --> 00:15:42.360
They liked the image of America that Nick was offering them, where of course they would help a young black man who approached them on the street and said he was in trouble.
他们喜欢尼克呈现给他们的那种美国形象：在这个形象中，当一个在街头接近他们并说自己遇到麻烦的年轻黑人向他们求助时，他们理所当然会伸出援手。

00:15:43.660 --> 00:15:48.610
And when the truth of the con came out, when Nick took that image away from them, they were furious.
而当骗局的真相大白、当尼克把那个形象从他们身上夺走时，他们愤怒了。

00:15:49.680 --> 00:15:57.160
There's a way in which you could think of Nick Ward as an agent or even an angel.
从某种角度来看，你可以把尼克·沃德看作一个特使，甚至是一个天使。

00:15:57.380 --> 00:16:04.580
He may not know it, and he may not be angelic, but he was something that suddenly entered our lives, and we had a chance to take a test.
他自己可能并不知道，他可能也并不像天使，但他突然闯入了我们的生活，给我们提供了一次接受考验的机会。

00:16:06.240 --> 00:16:08.120
Do the right thing or not do the right thing.
是做正确的事，还是不做正确的事。

00:16:08.440 --> 00:16:09.120
Good Samaritan.
好撒玛利亚人。

00:16:10.260 --> 00:16:14.940
And I think that at the point at which people forked over the money, they were doing the right thing.
我认为在人们掏出钱的那一刻，他们是在做正确的事。

00:16:16.080 --> 00:16:18.860
Their attitude after that showed that they failed the test.
但他们之后的态度表明，他们没有通过这场考验。

00:16:19.920 --> 00:16:23.980
The test, Gary says, was to figure out which kind of person you want to be.
加里说，这场考验是为了弄清楚你想成为哪种人。

00:16:24.590 --> 00:16:31.630
The kind who would give money to a stranger who claims to be in need, or the kind who turns their back on that stranger because they don't want to be conned.
是那种会给自称身处困境的陌生人钱的人，还是那种因为害怕被骗而对陌生人冷眼相待的人。

00:16:32.780 --> 00:16:35.300
Gary says he's not sorry he gave Nick the money, even today.
加里说，直到今天，他也不后悔给尼克钱。

00:16:36.190 --> 00:16:40.620
He says at the end of the day, if he has to choose, he'd rather be a sucker than a cynic.
他说归根结底，如果非要二选一，他宁愿当个傻子，也不愿做一个愤世嫉俗的人。

00:16:42.440 --> 00:17:37.160
There's a passage in Matthew where Jesus tells his followers, if someone comes before you hungry and you give him meat, or thirsty and you give him drink or naked and you clothe him what you do for the least of these you do for me stop it right now this guy is not a seducer and he is not Jesus Christ you gotta be kidding this is Matthew perks again the assistant DA who supervised Nick's prosecution and he was as you can tell a little alarmed at the direction of some of my questions he turned to me when I quoted this Bible verse to him and said you're not going to turn this guy into some kind of folk hero are you i'm telling you i mean he's a knucklehead he says his favorite part of the nick ward story is the ending because that's where he got to con nick towards the end of this plot is where i saw him describe where we are yeah it's a little bit
马太福音里有一段话，耶稣对追随者说，如果有人饥饿你给他饭吃，口渴你给他水喝，赤身露体你给他衣服穿，你们对这些最卑微的人做的事，就是对我做的事。行了，住嘴吧！这家伙既不是个勾引人的骗子，也不是耶稣基督，你开什么玩笑吧！这是马太·珀克斯，也就是负责监督起诉尼克的助理地方检察官。正如你所能想象的，他对我的某些提问的方向感到有点警惕。当我向他引用这段圣经经文时，他转向我说，你不会把这家伙变成某种平民英雄吧？我告诉你，我的意思是，他就是个大笨蛋。他说他最喜欢尼克·沃德故事的部分是结局，因为那是他把尼克给耍了的地方。在这个阴谋的尾声，正是我看到他描述我们所在的地方时，没错，这有点……

00:17:37.160 --> 00:17:46.340
this is locust street about two blocks from written house square beautiful church on the other side of the street there's brownstone stone offices and upscale stores and restaurants.
这里是蝗虫街，离里滕豪斯广场大约两个街区，街对面有一座漂亮的教堂，还有褐砂石办公楼、高档商店和餐馆。

00:17:47.680 --> 00:17:56.420
Matthew took me out for a walk along the route of his encounter with Nick, an encounter that eventually led to Nick being sent to prison for 19 months and then banished from Philadelphia.
马太带我沿着他与尼克相遇的路线散步，正是这次相遇，最终导致尼克被判入狱19个月，随后被驱逐出费城。

00:17:57.720 --> 00:17:59.660
Nick approached him on New Year's Eve a little before midnight.
尼克是在新年前夜午夜前不久接近他的。

00:18:00.480 --> 00:18:05.440
Matthew was on his way home, and Nick came up to him and started spinning his story, complete with heart-wrenching details.
当时马太正走在回家的路上，尼克朝他走来，开始编造他的故事，其中还包含着让人心碎的细节。

00:18:05.960 --> 00:18:38.740
He showed me a little cut above his eye and told me that he'd been robbed and his and everything was taken from him and he'd been injured and he hated Philadelphia and he had to get back to Massachusetts so I thought I would take a little time and do the people of Philadelphia favor and so what happened next we walked he asked me for $60 to get home I told him I didn't have enough money on me so I told him we could walk to an automatic teller machine what What machine did you go to?
他给我看他眼睛上方的一个小伤口，告诉我说他被抢劫了，他的所有东西都被抢走了，他还受了伤，他讨厌费城，他必须回马萨诸塞州。所以我想我会花点时间，为费城人民做件好事。那么接下来发生了什么？我们走着，他向我要60美元回家，我告诉他我身上带的钱不够，所以我告诉我们可以走到一台自动取款机去。你们去了哪台机器？我带你去看。就在回头的路上。我们拐上了17th街，也就是尼克和马太一起走过的那条路，他们当时都暗怀鬼胎，盘算着怎么骗对方。我让马太回忆一下，当时事情发生时他心里到底在想什么。我在想，我怎么这么走运啊。你当时是不是在刻意扮演那个容易上当受骗的傻瓜？当然是。你是怎么做到的？我完全知道他在找什么。我表现得好像对发生在他身上的事感到很难过，并且打算帮他一把。你讲到这里时在笑。你很享受这段回忆。马太当时的愉悦感是如此显而易见。他深深沉浸在那种将尼克完全玩弄于股掌之中的回忆里。他体验到了尼克每天必定体验到的那种感觉——完美骗局带来的快乐，那种确切知道如何拿捏一个人以达到目的的快感。那种因为比对方想象中更了解他们而带来的掌控感。所以就是这里，犯罪现场。这就是那台Mac取款机。没错。其实这是一个银行大厅。这是一个里面有Mac取款机的银行大厅。没错。你看，我们走进去了，我取了钱交给他，我还让他写下他的姓名和地址，我知道那是假的；而我则给了他我真实的姓名和地址，因为我想把那些证据从他身上没收过来，以证明以欺骗手段盗窃的构成要件，证明他承诺会还钱却根本没有打算还。当你们像这样交换地址时，你对他说什么了？我在煽动他。我告诉他我其实不该这么做，因为我得向妻子解释，所以我必须绝对确信他会还钱。当然，我当时根本没结婚。所以你为了抓住尼克·沃德，凭空捏造了一个配偶。没错。我和马太开始离开自动取款机。大约在一个街区外，他说了这样一番话。我身上还留着我给他的那三张20美元呢。你刚刚说什么？你还留着他从你那儿拿走的钱？对。什么，你把它们存起来了？对。在哪儿？嗯，就是，你知道的，放在我公寓里。你把它们放在什么特殊的地方了吗？比如放进一个盒子里？这属于不能录音的内容，这太诡异了。一点也不诡异，这是最棒的部分。这可是一位助理地方检察官啊，他经手过那么多比尼克·沃德重要得多的案件，为什么他偏要把三张20美元钞票用回形针夹着，放在他公寓的架子上呢？我来告诉你为什么。因为从陌生人手里拿钱太刺激了。这就是尼克·沃德给马太提供的一次机会。马太从尼克·沃德手里拿走了60美元。那是他自己的60美元，但他确实拿了，而且他很享受，他还把钱留着。等等，那几张20美元到底在哪儿？就在我公寓的架子上。放在你能看到的地方？嗯，我知道它们在那儿，又没装框什么的。不，这太诡异了。你居然把它们留下了。对。

00:18:38.900 --> 00:18:39.350
I'll show you.
我带你去看。

00:18:39.880 --> 00:18:40.640
It's on the way back.
就在回程的路上。

00:18:41.040 --> 00:18:47.360
We turned up 17th Street, exactly where Nick and Matthew had walked together, each secretly plotting to con the other.
我们拐上了第17街，也就是尼克和马修曾经一起走过的地方，当时他们各自都在暗中盘算着怎么骗对方。

00:18:47.600 --> 00:18:51.760
And I asked Matthew to remember exactly what he had been thinking while it was happening.
我让马修仔细回想当时事情发生时他心里到底在想什么。

00:18:52.440 --> 00:18:55.380
I'm thinking, I can't believe how lucky I am.
我当时想，我怎么这么走运。

00:18:56.760 --> 00:19:02.040
Are you trying to sort of play the part of the rube?
你当时是在假装扮演乡巴佬吗？

00:19:02.260 --> 00:19:02.740
Absolutely.
当然是。

00:19:03.660 --> 00:19:04.640
And how were you doing that?
那你当时是怎么做的？

00:19:04.680 --> 00:19:07.420
I knew exactly what he was looking for.
我太清楚他想要什么了。

00:19:09.780 --> 00:19:14.560
I acted like I felt bad about what happened to him and I was going to try and help him out.
我表现得好像为他的遭遇感到难过，并且打算帮他一把。

00:19:17.220 --> 00:19:18.520
You're grinning as you're telling this.
你讲到这里的时候在笑呢。

00:19:18.600 --> 00:19:20.220
You're enjoying remembering this.
你很享受这段回忆。

00:19:21.940 --> 00:19:23.880
Matthew's pleasure at that moment was so palpable.
马修当时的那种喜悦感简直显而易见。

00:19:24.450 --> 00:19:27.960
He was so inside this memory of having Nick completely in his power.
他完全沉浸在那段把尼克玩弄于股掌之间的记忆中。

00:19:29.680 --> 00:19:38.220
He got to experience what Nick must have experienced every day, the joy of a perfect deception, of knowing exactly how to play someone to get what you want.
他体验到了尼克每天肯定都在体验的那种感觉——完美骗局的快乐，那种精准拿捏某人以达到目的的快感。

00:19:38.640 --> 00:19:42.120
That sense of power that comes from knowing someone better than they can imagine.
那种因为对一个人的了解远超对方想象而带来的掌控感。

00:19:42.520 --> 00:19:44.160
So this is it, the scene of the crime.
就是这里，犯罪现场。

00:19:45.060 --> 00:19:46.300
This is the Mac machine.
这就是自动取款机。

00:19:46.580 --> 00:19:46.900
That's right.
没错。

00:19:47.680 --> 00:19:48.800
It's a bank lobby, actually.
其实是个银行大厅。

00:19:48.800 --> 00:19:50.680
It's a bank lobby with Mac machines in it.
一个里面有自动取款机的银行大厅。

00:19:50.820 --> 00:19:50.880
Right.
对。

00:19:51.160 --> 00:20:12.420
See, we went in there, I withdrew the money and gave it to him, and I made him write down his name and address, which I knew to be fictitious, and I gave him my correct name and address because I wanted that evidence to get confiscated from him, to show the elements of theft by deception, to show that he promised to pay me back and had no intention of doing it.
你看，我们进去了，我取了钱交给他，我还让他写下他的姓名和地址（我知道那是假的），而我给了他我真实的姓名和地址，因为我想让那些证据从他身上被没收，以此来证明虚假借口诈骗的要素，证明他承诺会还钱却根本没有这个打算。

00:20:12.940 --> 00:20:17.980
And as you guys were exchanging addresses like this, what were you saying to him?
当你们像这样交换地址的时候，你对他说什么了？

00:20:19.400 --> 00:20:20.240
I was egging him on.
我在拱火。

00:20:20.380 --> 00:20:27.720
I told him that I really shouldn't be doing it because I'd have to explain it to my wife, so I had to be absolutely sure he was going to pay me back.
我告诉他我其实不该这么做，因为我得向我妻子解释，所以我必须绝对确信他会还钱。

00:20:27.920 --> 00:20:29.600
And, of course, I wasn't married at the time.
当然，我那时候根本没结婚。

00:20:30.100 --> 00:20:34.880
So you, to catch Nick Ward, you invented a spouse.
所以为了抓尼克·沃德，你凭空捏造了一个配偶。

00:20:35.300 --> 00:20:35.780
That's correct.
没错。

00:20:38.940 --> 00:20:41.000
Matthew and I started walking away from the ATM machine.
我和马修开始离开自动取款机。

00:20:41.200 --> 00:20:42.980
And about a block away, he said this.
大约一个街区外，他说了这样的话。

00:20:43.300 --> 00:20:44.820
I still have the 320s I gave him.
我手里还留着我给他的那些20美元面值的钞票。

00:20:46.780 --> 00:20:47.540
What did you just say?
你刚才说什么？

00:20:47.680 --> 00:20:49.260
You still have the money he took from you?
你还留着他拿走你的钱？

00:20:49.360 --> 00:20:49.580
Yeah.
对。

00:20:50.820 --> 00:20:51.740
What, you have it saved?
什么，你把它存起来了？

00:20:52.140 --> 00:20:52.340
Yeah.
对。

00:20:53.060 --> 00:20:53.460
Where?
在哪儿？

00:20:53.840 --> 00:20:55.740
Well, just, you know, sitting in my apartment.
呃，就是，你知道的，放在我公寓里。

00:20:56.280 --> 00:20:57.550
Do you have them in a special place?
你把它放在什么特殊的地方了吗？

00:20:59.520 --> 00:21:47.180
like what in a box that's off the record that's too weird it's not weird at all it's the best part this is an assistant da he has had so many cases much more important than nick ward so why on earth does he have 320s sitting on a shelf in his apartment in a paper clip i'll tell you why because it's thrilling to take money from strangers and that is what nick ward gave matthew the chance to do matthew took sixty dollars from nick ward it was his own sixty dollars but he took it and he enjoyed it and he's keeping it no wait where are those 20s just on a shelf in my apartment and you where you can see them well i know they're there they're not framed or anything.
比如说放在一个不为人知的盒子里？这也太奇怪了吧。一点也不奇怪，这才是最精彩的部分。这可是一位助理地方检察官，他经手过那么多比尼克沃德重要得多的案件，那他凭什么要把几张20块钱的钞票放在公寓的书架上，还用回形针夹着？我来告诉你为什么，因为从陌生人手里拿钱太刺激了。这就是尼克·沃德给马修的机会。马修从尼克·沃德那里拿走了六十美元。那是他自己的六十美元，但他就是拿了，而且他很享受，他还留着它。等一下，那几张20块钱到底在哪？就放在我公寓的书架上。在哪能看到它们的地方吗？嗯，我知道它们在那，又没装框什么的。

00:21:48.180 --> 00:21:49.300
No, that's too weird.
不，这太奇怪了。

00:21:49.540 --> 00:21:51.560
You just kept them.
你居然把它们留下了。

00:21:52.160 --> 00:21:52.480
Yeah.
对。

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:01.700
Nancy Updike, here in Chicago.
这里是芝加哥的南希·厄普代克。

00:22:16.860 --> 00:23:17.260
I've got no time to be alone Some are coming at me all the time Your baby think I lose my mind Cause I'm stranded on my own Stranded far from home All right I'm riding on a midnight train But everybody just looks the same The sub is like a third of reflection I'm lying straight up, got no direction And I'm stranded on my own Stranded, far from home All right Stranded, I'm so far from home Stranded, yeah, I'm on my own Coming up, Sarah Val with a legitimate businessman man who takes large sums of money from people and they love him for it.
我没有时间独处 总有人不断来找我 你的宝贝以为我失去了理智 因为我孤身一人搁浅 搁浅在远离家乡的地方 好吧 我正坐上深夜的列车 但每个人看起来都一样 地铁就像是折射出第三种影像 我笔直地躺着 失去了方向 孤身一人搁浅 搁浅在远离家乡的地方 好吧 搁浅了 我离家是如此之遥 搁浅了 是啊 我孤身一人 接下来，莎拉·瓦尔将带来一位合法的商人，他从人们那里拿走大笔金钱，而人们却对此爱不释手。

00:23:17.880 --> 00:23:21.920
And writer Stephen Glass takes large amounts of money from poor people as a telephone psychic.
作家斯蒂芬·格拉斯则通过电话占卜，从穷人那里骗取大量金钱。

00:23:22.920 --> 00:23:27.100
That's in a minute from Public Radio International when our program continues.
节目继续，稍后由国际公共广播电台为您播出。

00:23:52.940 --> 00:23:53.280
We'll be right back.
我们马上回来。

00:24:28.840 --> 00:24:29.980
This is American Life.
这里是《美国生活》。

00:24:30.020 --> 00:24:30.520
I'm Ira Glass.
我是艾拉·格拉斯。

00:24:30.930 --> 00:24:37.600
Each week on our program, of course, we choose a theme, invite a variety of different writers and performers and reporters to take a whack at that theme.
当然，我们节目的每周惯例是，确定一个主题，邀请各种不同的作家、表演者和记者来围绕这个主题各抒己见。

00:24:38.440 --> 00:24:41.020
Today's program, taking money from strangers.
今天的节目主题是：从陌生人手里拿钱。

00:24:41.020 --> 00:24:41.160
years.
年。

00:24:42.180 --> 00:24:46.680
Michael Schuman is an attorney in New York, and part of his practice is defending people who are accused of fraud.
迈克尔·舒曼是纽约的一名律师，他的业务之一就是为被指控犯有欺诈罪的人辩护。

00:24:48.020 --> 00:24:53.820
And he says that fraud cases are interesting to him because it shows a side of human behavior that is just interesting.
他说，欺诈案对他的吸引力在于，它展现了人类行为中引人入胜的一面。

00:24:54.000 --> 00:24:54.800
It's just inherently interesting.
这种吸引力是与生俱来的。

00:24:54.900 --> 00:24:59.580
It's people betting on a promise in these cases, people betting on a dream.
在这些案件中，人们是在为一个承诺下注，人们在为梦想下注。

00:25:00.240 --> 00:25:02.700
Americans like to play the long shot.
美国人喜欢押注于那些胜率极低但回报巨大的事。

00:25:03.640 --> 00:25:13.720
So when a salesman comes around and says, this piece of land is going to be worth triple its value in three or four years.
所以当一个推销员跑来对你说，这块土地三四年后价值会翻三番。

00:25:14.220 --> 00:25:25.080
I think people know that there's a big risk involved in that and the chances are it doesn't, it's not necessarily going to work out that way.
我想人们心里清楚，这里面存在巨大的风险，而且很有可能事情不会像预期那样发展。

00:25:26.300 --> 00:25:28.480
But they want to take the chance.
但他们还是想冒这个险。

00:25:33.650 --> 00:25:35.600
All right, you can tell which side of these cases he's on.
好吧，你大概能看出他是站在这些案件的哪一边了。

00:25:35.820 --> 00:25:36.840
but he has a point.
不过他说的也有道理。

00:25:38.160 --> 00:25:39.780
This is the plot of a thousand Hollywood films.
成千上万的好莱坞电影都是这个套路。

00:25:40.180 --> 00:25:42.380
This is the story that runs our country.
这就是驱动我们国家的现实故事。

00:25:42.420 --> 00:25:47.340
This is the nobody who sets off after the least likely thing in the world and gets it.
这就是那个一无所有的人，追逐着世上最不可能实现的目标，最后却成功了的故事。

00:25:48.040 --> 00:25:48.740
That is this story.
这就是这个故事。

00:25:50.040 --> 00:25:51.420
And it is a deep part of our culture.
这也是我们文化中根深蒂固的一部分。

00:25:52.720 --> 00:26:01.920
Well, Sarah Val has this story about a businessman who is very successful getting people to dream and taking away money from strangers.
好吧，莎拉·瓦尔带来了这样一个故事：关于一个非常成功的商人，他如何让人做梦，并从陌生人那里拿走金钱。

00:26:02.970 --> 00:26:12.020
I once had a job as the manager of a San Francisco gallery owned by an infamous New York antiquarian print and map dealer named Grammar Raider.
我曾经在旧金山的一家画廊担任经理，这家画廊属于一个臭名昭著的纽约古董版画和地图商，名叫格雷厄姆·雷德。

00:26:12.020 --> 00:26:31.840
I hung gigantic Audubon pelicans and delicate little 18th century prints of lilies and roses on the wall, wrote gushy letters to collectors about 400-year-old maps of North America, and helped the sales staff try and push chromolithographs of the Grand Canyon onto unsuspecting housewives.
我在墙上挂过巨大的奥杜邦鹈鹕版画，以及精致的18世纪百合花和玫瑰花小版画，给收藏家写过肉麻的信，推销那些有400年历史的北美地图，还帮销售人员把大峡谷的彩色石版画强推给毫无防备的家庭主妇。

00:26:32.860 --> 00:26:35.840
It was one of those boy-do-I-need-a-job kind of jobs.
那正是一种“天哪我太需要一份工作了”类型的工作。

00:26:40.290 --> 00:26:46.560
Grammarator was the kind of fascinating boss whom employees gossip about with a mix of adulation and dread.
格雷厄姆·雷德就是那种迷人的老板，员工们在八卦他时，总是夹杂着崇拜与敬畏。

00:26:47.380 --> 00:26:55.620
One of those go-go-go guys whose ambition you could admire while at the same time hoping you weren't the one who answered the phone when he called.
那种风风火火的干劲十足的人，你既会钦佩他的野心，同时又祈祷他打电话来时接电话的不是自己。

00:26:56.820 --> 00:26:57.580
Gram-a-rater.
格雷厄姆·雷德。

00:26:58.160 --> 00:26:59.200
The Grammarator.
雷德终结者。

00:26:59.400 --> 00:27:04.140
You almost wanted to say his full name every time to make it sound even more like the Terminator.
你几乎每次都想连名带姓地叫他，好让他的名字听起来更像是“终结者”。

00:27:05.220 --> 00:27:15.120
My My favorite story about the Grammarator, one printed in the New Yorker so presumably it may even be true, says that Graham once desired a map owned by a friend.
关于格雷厄姆，我最喜欢的一个故事曾被刊登在《纽约客》杂志上，所以据推测它甚至可能是真的：有一次，格雷厄姆看中了一位朋友收藏的一幅地图。

00:27:15.580 --> 00:27:31.660
Graham begged the guy for it, told the guy that he needed the map, that he loved this map so much he wanted to hang it over his bed, that it would be the first thing he saw when he got up in the morning and the last thing he saw when he went to sleep at night, that he wanted to conceive his children under it.
格雷厄姆对那家伙死缠烂打，告诉对方他多么需要这幅地图，他太喜欢这幅地图了，以至于想把它挂在床头，这是他早晨醒来看到的第一个东西，也是晚上睡觉前看到的最后一个东西，他甚至想在这幅地图下方孕育他的孩子。

00:27:31.760 --> 00:27:35.000
At least that's the version of the story his employees tell each other.
至少这是他的员工们互相转述的版本。

00:27:35.700 --> 00:27:45.840
The friend was so touched he sold it to the Grammarator, who promptly called the friend the very next day bragging that he'd sold it, and for a tidy profit at that.
那位朋友深受感动，于是把它卖给了格雷厄姆，结果格雷厄姆第二天就打电话给这位朋友，大肆吹嘘自己把它转手了，而且还赚了一笔可观的利润。

00:27:52.300 --> 00:27:53.900
Map dealers just aren't like that.
地图商可从来不是这样的人。

00:27:54.040 --> 00:28:01.980
As a group, they tend to be polite, bookish, and don't inspire comparisons to Arnold Schwarzenegger or any other mythic pop figure.
总体而言，他们往往彬彬有礼、充满书卷气，绝不会让人把他们和阿诺德·施瓦辛格或其他任何神话流行人物联系起来。

00:28:02.760 --> 00:28:06.360
Graham is the map dealer's Michael Milken, their Elvis Presley.
格雷厄姆就是地图商里的迈克尔·米尔肯，是他们的猫王。

00:28:07.540 --> 00:28:15.580
In financial terms, he put antiquarian maps on the map, and he popularized them like no one ever had, through sheer charisma.
从财务角度来看，他在地图界占据了一席之地，并且凭借着纯粹的个人魅力，将古董地图普及到了前所未有的高度。

00:28:16.400 --> 00:28:22.120
The antiquarian map market before Grammarator was a fairly sophisticated market.
在格雷厄姆出现之前，古董地图市场是一个相当成熟的市场。

00:28:22.120 --> 00:28:28.240
The people that collected had in-depth knowledge and understanding.
那些收藏的人都具备深入的知识和理解。

00:28:29.090 --> 00:28:37.220
I guess the effect that I had is I brought map collecting to a lot more people who perhaps in the beginning were not as sophisticated.
我想我所产生的影响是，我把地图收藏带给了更多的人，他们在刚开始时或许并没有那么专业。

00:28:39.080 --> 00:28:42.840
And the prices have gone up, and I get blamed a lot.
价格上涨了，而我成了众矢之的。

00:28:43.780 --> 00:28:51.780
What Graham isn't telling you is that he functions as the messiah of the map biz, or it's antichrist, depending on your point of view.
格雷厄姆没有告诉你的是，他在地图界既扮演着救世主的角色，也扮演着魔鬼的角色，这取决于你的看法。

00:28:52.120 --> 00:29:05.760
The entire industry can be divided, B.A., before a raider, when many historical maps sold for a few hundred dollars, and A.A., after a raider, when the same maps began commanding tens of thousands of dollars.
整个行业可以划分为，古图贩子出现前（B.A.）——当时许多历史地图只卖几百美元，以及古图贩子出现后（A.A.）——同样的地图开始卖到数万美元的高价。

00:29:06.600 --> 00:29:11.580
His beginnings in the early 1970s sound so American, so go west, young man.
他在20世纪70年代初的起步经历听起来非常美国化，充满了“年轻人，去西部吧”的色彩。

00:29:12.420 --> 00:29:15.100
He was dealing maps out of his dorm room at Yale.
他当时在耶鲁大学的宿舍里倒卖地图。

00:29:15.880 --> 00:29:20.120
And then there's the story about how his mother ruined Baby's first big sale.
还有一个故事，讲的是他母亲是如何搞砸了小贝（Baby）的第一笔大生意的。

00:29:20.120 --> 00:29:36.800
What happened was I'd been working the whole summer buying maps of Penobscot Bay for Thomas Watson, who was then the chairman and chief operating officer of IBM, and he had never met me.
事情是这样的，那个夏天我一直在为托马斯·沃森购买描绘佩诺布斯科特湾的地图，托马斯·沃森当时是IBM的董事长兼首席运营官，而他从来没见过我。

00:29:36.860 --> 00:29:44.660
I had met him through by phone, and I went ahead and found all these fabulous maps that showed Penobscot Bay.
我是通过电话和他认识的，然后我找齐了所有这些展现佩诺布斯科特湾的绝妙地图。

00:29:45.460 --> 00:29:49.280
So at the end of the summer, I'd amassed this really incredible collection.
所以在夏天结束时，我积攒了这样一个极其惊人的收藏。

00:29:50.220 --> 00:29:51.780
I was living with my parents at the time.
我当时和父母住在一起。

00:29:51.820 --> 00:29:53.640
I was 23 years old.
我23岁。

00:29:54.440 --> 00:30:02.980
And when Mr. Watson called, my mother not knowing who he was or why he was calling, my mother said, I'm sorry, you'll have to use the children's phone.
当沃森先生打来电话时，我母亲不知道他是谁，也不知道他为什么打电话，我母亲说：“对不起，你得去用儿童电话。”

00:30:03.680 --> 00:30:07.820
Mr. Watson hung up and didn't talk to me again for two years.
沃森先生挂了电话，两年没再跟我说话。

00:30:08.620 --> 00:30:16.960
In the years since, Graham Arader has acquired not just one, but many, many of his own telephone lines at the galleries he owns across the country.
在此后的岁月里，格雷厄姆·阿拉德不仅在全国各地自己拥有的画廊里拥有了一部电话线，而是拥有了许许多多部。

00:30:17.860 --> 00:30:24.900
He's a successful salesman, the most successful salesman in his market for two reasons, kindness and optimism.
他是一名成功的推销员，是他那个市场上最成功的推销员，原因有二：善良和乐观。

00:30:26.180 --> 00:30:27.020
Let's start with kindness.
我们先从善良说起。

00:30:31.340 --> 00:30:36.940
In some ways, the getting money ways, Graham Arader is one of the nicest guys you'll ever meet.
在某些方面，也就是赚钱的方面，格雷厄姆·阿拉德是你见过的最好的人之一。

00:30:37.220 --> 00:30:41.040
Now, some people, people who actually know him, are going to laugh at that.
现在，有些人，也就是真正了解他的人，听到这话要笑了。

00:30:41.760 --> 00:30:44.360
Graham doesn't have a nice guy rep with his competitors.
在竞争对手眼中，格雷厄姆可没有什么老好人的名声。

00:30:45.260 --> 00:30:52.720
This is the man who, at auction, has successfully intimidated the unthinkably wealthy Getty Museum into backing down on bids.
这个人曾在拍卖会上成功威慑了富得流油的盖蒂博物馆，迫使其在竞标时退缩。

00:30:53.840 --> 00:30:54.060
That's scary.
这太可怕了。

00:30:54.980 --> 00:30:57.520
I mean, the Grammarator's rich, but he's not that rich.
我的意思是，阿拉德很富有，但也没富到那种程度。

00:31:06.040 --> 00:31:08.820
Granted, he's only nice to you if he wants something out of you.
当然，他只有在想从你身上得到什么的时候才会对你好。

00:31:09.080 --> 00:31:12.640
but Grammarator wants something from a lot of people all the time.
但阿拉德总是想从很多人身上得到东西。

00:31:12.920 --> 00:31:16.420
His secret for getting money from strangers boils down to this.
他从陌生人那里弄到钱的秘诀归结为一点。

00:31:17.240 --> 00:31:18.320
Strangers don't give you money.
陌生人是不会给你钱的。

00:31:18.500 --> 00:31:19.120
Friends do.
朋友才会。

00:31:20.250 --> 00:31:22.720
Relationships, he used to tell us in sales seminars.
他过去常常在销售研讨会上对我们说，人际关系。

00:31:23.320 --> 00:31:24.680
Selling comes from relationships.
销售源于人际关系。

00:31:25.420 --> 00:31:31.360
He bought the most perfect Bodmer dog dancer with perfect original color and full margins.
他买下了最完美的博德默养狗人画像，有着完美的原始色彩和完整的页边。

00:31:32.120 --> 00:31:38.900
This is Gram in a cab on the way to Sotheby's with one of his best clients, A guy who looks to be, oh, about 50 years old.
这是格雷厄姆坐在去苏富比拍卖行的出租车上，身边坐着他最好的客户之一，一个看起来大约50岁左右的男人。

00:32:07.020 --> 00:32:12.440
Graham carries around a small but well-used toolbox of superlatives, which he hammers into everything.
格雷厄姆随身带着一个虽小但很常用的最高级词汇工具箱，他把这些词砸进每一句话里。

00:32:13.260 --> 00:32:18.560
Words like fabulous and greatest and finest and his favorite best.
诸如“极好”、“最伟大”、“最精致”之类的词，还有他最喜欢的“最好”。

00:32:19.240 --> 00:32:21.020
Jeez, it's a great dream.
天哪，这是一个伟大的梦想。

